<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boule</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vouli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The boule was a council that ran the daily affairs of a city in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>; originally a council of nobles advising a king, it was hereditary in some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchies</a> and chosen by lot for one year in democracies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> is the best documented: under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> it had 400 members, 100 from each of the four tribes, and after 508 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> made it a council of 500, 50 from each of his ten new tribes, chosen by lot from male citizens aged 30 or more. It set the agenda of the Assembly and carried out its decisions, and after the reforms of Ephialtes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> it took over many functions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Areopagus</a>. The name survives as the Vouli, the Parliament of modern Greece.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An inscription of about 575–550 BCE refers to a popular council (<b>boule</b> demosie) that met monthly, heard appeals and handled public business, alongside magistrates called demarchoi and basileis; it is one of the earliest pieces of evidence for a council of this kind outside Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Hellenistic Ephesus was governed by a council (<b>boule</b>) and assembly (demos) that passed decrees, granted citizenship and honoured benefactors, while Lysimachus created a council of elders, the gerousia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its business was prepared by the Council of Five Hundred (<b>boule</b>), fifty men from each of the ten tribes chosen by lot for a year.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
